# Distinct interpretation prompts; all feelings remain hypotheses, never invented interviews.
fit={
'maya':'Her care work gives her a relevant career tree and a healing identity. The shift pattern also leaves accepted calendar goals unfinished. A useful next-action view should distinguish that from losing earned progress; neither the missed rota nor her old Sentinel level should be framed as a personal failure.',
'tomas':'This is a meaningful non-IT technical path: electrical learning unlocks the trades tool while class work develops Artificer. His professional DEX exam and the INT focus of his class are different checks. The app should explain that split and should never imply that a game level licenses electrical work.',
'ada':'Teaching preparation is rewarded strongly, but a high player/profession level does not substitute for Bard class work. Her Maestro aspiration can remain distant despite an impressive guild card. The app needs to show the next relevant class commitment, rather than only recommending more work in the already fast-growing career.',
'ruth':'Seasonal availability matters more than a daily streak. Her gathered-resource tool fits the farming fantasy, while the model retains all earlier work through harvest. A plan that shows which quests fit the remaining season would serve her better than treating every calendar period as interchangeable.',
'evan':'Finance has a maintained tree and a concrete reconciliation deliverable, rather than borrowing a networking syllabus. Tax-season absence pauses receipts without erasing them. Archmage still depends on class-specific progress; a large accounting portfolio is valuable but is not a substitute for that gate.',
'leila':'Engineer is a successful advanced identity, but its high level does not satisfy Grand Artificer’s Artificer-level prerequisite. This exposes a vertical-progression design question: should relevant advanced training count toward a lineage, or should the UI clearly recommend returning to the predecessor class?',
'graham':'His Sage path benefits from deliberately training both scholarship and Christian reflection. The trace makes the cost of that mixed goal visible. Reaching the class is a game outcome; neither the research work sample nor the generic mental checks establish academic expertise.',
'sofia':'Legal practice and a precision-focused class can coexist. Her case materials are fictional, and the legal field utility offers a distinct Adventure use. Clear labels matter: a legal work record, a class level and a generic exam are different forms of evidence.',
'marcus':'Community work, faith practice and a healer identity fit the app’s mission closely. The monthly service event is an explicitly assumed group activity. A stronger class and large WIS value should reward persistence without presenting spiritual maturity or professional judgment as a validated numerical score.',
'imani':'The creative tree and support class give revisions a visible place in the game. Her continued Minstrel training is meaningful even though her declared goal stops at that advanced class. Future prestige guidance should preserve those levels and explain predecessor requirements without forcing an unexplained restart.',
'ben':'The travel pattern exposes the cost of commitments that assume a predictable week. His listening quest is a separate modeled group activity, not another claim for the same sales block. A short, actionable return screen would be more useful than a streak warning after travel.',
'nadia':'This is a viable career-first player with a nearly untouched combat class. The schedule usually spends the available block on vocation work, leaving no time for Sentinel practice. The UI should show that deliberate tradeoff and banked class XP; a class level of 1 does not mean the account has made no progress.',
'hugo':'Paladin requires both the named predecessor levels and higher STR/WIS values. His kitchen career mainly grows DEX, so those requirements are not automatically satisfied by professional progress. The long route is real in this scenario; meeting the class-level prerequisites alone is not the end of the promotion.',
'chloe':'Measurement and quality work have their own deliverable and utility. Duelist gives an early focused identity while her inspection career continues independently. The game should keep accuracy and reviewed work prominent, rather than reward faster tapping as if it proved manufacturing competence.',
'owen':'His short windows still produce retained progress, but splitting them across conditioning and career work misses many calendar thresholds. That is a schedule/reward issue to inspect, not evidence that the work is worthless. The logistics tool is available once he deliberately reviews skills on a day he actually uses the app.',
'priya':'The trace shows the requested vertical pattern: earned Warrior history, a Champion trial, then Champion level 1 beside a much higher player level. Physical exam plateaus can delay the next milestone even with banked XP. Earlier mastery and the later maintenance route should be easy to inspect.',
'celeste':'Guild Strategist combines leadership, knowledge and predecessor training. Her path includes more than simply accumulating management XP. A class-goal plan should explain the contribution of each prerequisite and keep the retained Bard/Marshal records visible after promotion.',
'daniel':'Operations work produces a practical process artifact and a guild-docket utility. Career growth can be much faster than the selected class. The useful feedback is what each block advanced and which requirement remains, rather than a generic total-XP celebration.',
'mei':'Elementalist is an early, accessible advanced identity because this fixture begins with a strong authored reasoning result. Later gains come from modeled work and milestone outcomes. The tiny game check is not an IQ score, and the financial model’s quality remains a scenario assumption.',
'jordan':'His credential is explicitly assumed and self-recorded. The severe issue comes afterward: Network Weaver requests are assigned to the profession scope, so repeated intended class commissions never advance class work. This is a reproduced request-classification defect, not an exam choice or lack of effort.',
'aisha':'Her later goal deliberately moves beyond Arcanist toward Archmage. The app retains the advanced class but requires the stated Scholar lineage. This is a useful case for showing a clear promotion plan, including whether advanced practice contributes to an earlier prerequisite.',
'felix':'Authorized security learning and precision training lead to a Shadowblade path without making the occupation a combat prerequisite. His on-call break pauses progress, then the same account resumes. The model uses ordinary encounters; it does not demonstrate that this build is balanced against every Elite hunt.',
'wendy':'Business Analysis is relevant to her existing communication strengths, and Battle Analyst is an advanced goal with a profession-category gate. The career artifact is a process map and acceptance criteria. It is not interchangeable with a generic coding lab, even though the current reward structure is similar.',
'theo':'This is the clearest exam-friction case. The primary run deliberately refuses exams while continuing the same work. His career tree and player level still advance, but the certified class/profession levels stop at 4. The matched review run shows why an actionable exam prompt matters without removing the gate.',
'helen':'The modest numerical result is compatible with her modest goal. She can keep short walks, learning and Scripture practice without becoming an intensive player. Clear terminology, a readable card and attainable commitments matter more to this persona than a larger combat rotation.',
'luis':'A capable initial assessment allows Monk, but the Warpriest aspiration requires a different retained class history. His move is deliberate goal pursuit, not loss of the original class. The account illustrates why starting fitness, professional learning and faith-oriented progression should be distinguishable.',
'paige':'The model treats two incomplete strength attempts as unfinished work and later preserves the career change. That is accurate reward behavior, but it also exposes the current absence of a separate earned-progress record for partial sessions. Her later career focus can grow while Bard remains largely untrained. The detailed creative assignments are authored inputs; the app’s generic career-changer request is less specific than its Analyst job-application branch.',
'quinn':'A software vocation can earn technology-tree progress while the selected profession remains UX Designer. Engineer still checks the profession category. The same vocation receipts also add XP to the current profession, so credit destinations need clearer design and labeling rather than an unexplained locked-class message.',
'irene':'Her outdoor detour retains earlier scholarship and adds another class record. Returning to a Sage goal can leave several exam/lineage steps open at once. A chronological Journey is useful here, but a concise current-next-step plan would make the preserved history easier to act on.',
'darius':'A precision profession and Rogue lineage reinforce the game’s DEX direction. That is an authored category mapping, not proof that CNC work raises every component of physical dexterity. His larger weekend budget also makes the class route easier than a weekday-only comparison would imply.',
'elena':'The career change keeps Baker history while giving Graphic Designer its own level and a second utility. The new role level is lower than the no-change counterfactual, as expected when practice is split between careers. The app should present that as retained breadth rather than erased progress.',
'noah':'This is a sustained faith-and-service route with a High Cleric identity and useful community work. Group service is separate from solo daily tasks. Large WIS values are RPG progression; the app should continue to keep spiritual worth outside the scoring claim.',
}
